#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105539 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3105e8eb-010f-542b-9e40-12fda6ea00e7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Standard Matrix of a Stretch Transformation Given a Vector and its Image"}] :question/id #uuid "ffbe74ea-33e6-5998-8692-ed5d7bd0c30d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5450" :question/problem "A linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis. Given that $\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-16 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ what is the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we are told that $\\mathbf{T}$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis.\n\nComparing the $x$- and $y$-components of $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v})$, we note the following:\n\n- the $x$-component of $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v})$ is $4$ times the $x$-component of $\\mathbf{v}$, while\n- the $y$-components of $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v})$ are the same.\n\nSo, we conclude that $\\mathbf{T}$ represents a stretch of scale factor $4$ in the $x$-direction.\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105540 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "be42e4b3-4b15-589c-a7e1-23cc07083aff" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Shear or Stretch Transformation Given the Images of the Standard Basis Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "83ef303a-c25c-5cd5-8ab7-2c14cb05d433" :question/math-academy-id "e-5454" :question/problem "Consider the standard basis $\\{\\mathbf{i}, \\mathbf{j} \\}$ of $\\,\\mathbb{R}^2$. If $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation such that $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{i})=\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{j})=\\mathbf{j}$ then, what is the geometric interpretation of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the unit square spanned by the vectors $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{j}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1358/Source/Images/c28904112781381da0083548d8c2dbbf.png)\n\nNow, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{i})=\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{j})=\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nwe can draw the image of the square under the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1358/Source/Images/e46ae3a679baae89ff308c327360ee8e.png)\n\nTherefore, the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ represents a vertical shear with a shear factor of $2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105541 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c36ce626-1562-51f8-b3fd-d7dc7ce8659c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Shear or Stretch Transformation Represented by a Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "4d800cd6-01bd-561a-97cb-1bd672c019c7" :question/math-academy-id "e-5455" :question/problem "What linear transformation is represented by the following matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "We can find the images of the standard basis vectors $\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf j$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(i) &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} = i \\\\\nT(j) &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} = 2i + j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix represents a *horizontal* shear with a shear factor of $2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105542 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "69bac3fa-d409-5c16-87b2-383423e8d754" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Square Under a Shear or Stretch Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "043f45e2-97eb-540d-af0a-b04edb9ce60a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5456" :question/problem "Let $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ be the standard matrix of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$. What is the image of the square $R$ (shown above) under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1358/Source/Images/8e226d694c513c348b7136c96c29a52b.png)" :question/worked-solution "Looking at the matrix $T$, we can see that $\\mathbf{T}$ represents a vertical shear of shear factor $-1$. Therefore, the image of the square $R$ under $\\mathbf{T}$ is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1358/Source/Images/6c7707ce3c667c5692b4312d72bc9578.png)\n\n**Alternative method:**\n\nWe can check that the vertices of $R$ are mapped to the vertices of the parallelogram shown above as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nT(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nT(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}) &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nT(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}) &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105960 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c36ce626-1562-51f8-b3fd-d7dc7ce8659c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Shear or Stretch Transformation Represented by a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81b8ead8-163c-55e6-afa2-95af3681a7e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A horizontal shear with a shear factor of $10$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102008} {:answer/id #uuid "9095931b-ff08-5a54-a2fb-955f264244c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch of scale factor $10$ in the $x$-direction" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102009} {:answer/id #uuid "955f0cc5-43f6-5a0e-af94-704b0c7a58c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A vertical shear with a shear factor of $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102010} {:answer/id #uuid "b0555da6-a04e-5144-979c-b0a96959fdd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch of scale factor $10$ in the $y$-direction" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102011} {:answer/id #uuid "273e7475-d054-5f26-b9f1-7bb8aa9231b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A vertical shear with a shear factor of $10$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102012}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81b8ead8-163c-55e6-afa2-95af3681a7e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A horizontal shear with a shear factor of $10$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102008} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e5573e5-02b4-5a74-83f5-8f8e0b6b8adb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102007}] :question/id #uuid "6ec67f6b-b339-5863-9fd2-be6322d61819" :question/math-academy-id "q-12044" :question/problem "What linear transformation is represented by the following matrix\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106235 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "be42e4b3-4b15-589c-a7e1-23cc07083aff" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Shear or Stretch Transformation Given the Images of the Standard Basis Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c683b49-f6bb-5acb-9d17-84206cfc5a1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A vertical shear with a shear factor of $1.5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103920} {:answer/id #uuid "9e2106a7-b49c-543e-a322-1a9fa6bf6e2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch of scale factor $2.5$ in the $y$-direction" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103921} {:answer/id #uuid "2639659d-85d9-5faa-be2f-dfbf3d48ab9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch of scale factor $1.5$ in the $x$-direction" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103922} {:answer/id #uuid "3184ea3b-c017-511e-b474-90e157e3cd20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A horizontal shear with a shear factor of $1.5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103923} {:answer/id #uuid "68e3cb33-d35c-56e4-b8a7-21f193ac1a02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch of scale factor $1.5$ in the $y$-direction" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103924}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3184ea3b-c017-511e-b474-90e157e3cd20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A horizontal shear with a shear factor of $1.5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103923} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f4476f2-f0bb-5620-8663-b9f0e1397b7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103919}] :question/id #uuid "d5441e20-477a-5032-8e85-180d271e5aa1" :question/math-academy-id "q-38052" :question/problem "Consider the standard basis ${i, j}$ of $R^{2}$. If $T$ is a linear transformation such that $T(i) = i$ and $T(j) = 1.5i + j$, then what is the geometric interpretation of the transformation $T$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106236 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "69bac3fa-d409-5c16-87b2-383423e8d754" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Square Under a Shear or Stretch Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "869570ef-da66-5f87-b516-a2bd3252566c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1358/Source/Images/q-38066-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103926} {:answer/id #uuid "07b11424-f45d-566a-9c13-cc4b0f6b6d10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/L